1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,909
Same robot.

2
00:00:00,909 --> 00:00:01,735
Same task.

3
00:00:01,735 --> 00:00:04,049
One run succeeds, one fails.

4
00:00:04,049 --> 00:00:10,825
Before I ship this policy to a tiny Jetson, how do I know which model I can trust?

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:21,322
For quantization, we need to pick the candidate that has earned its next test, not an approval.

6
00:00:21,322 --> 00:00:25,075
That's why robot researchers need Quant Gate.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:33,825
This is an S O one oh one arm running an A C T policy, Action Chunking with Transformers, on a Jetson Orin Nano Super.

8
00:00:33,825 --> 00:00:40,762
We taught it with one hundred and twenty teleoperated demonstrations, and trained it with Nebius Jobs.

9
00:00:40,762 --> 00:00:53,344
From that one policy, we built twenty-six candidate engines with TensorRT: full precision, half precision, and twenty-four int eight builds that differ only in how they were calibrated.

10
00:00:53,344 --> 00:00:55,248
Every real trial is blinded.

11
00:00:55,248 --> 00:01:02,729
The operator never knows which engine is running, and the results stay sealed until all the trials are logged.

12
00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:07,150
That way, no candidate gets picked after we have seen how it did.

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:19,721
QuantGate puts three things side by side for every candidate: what it costs on the real device, how it scored offline, and what it did on the real arm.

14
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:23,337
Training and the offline audit ran on Nebius Jobs.

15
00:01:23,337 --> 00:01:26,375
The engines run as TensorRT on the Jetson.

16
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,322
Measuring changed the decision.

17
00:01:29,322 --> 00:01:36,758
On this Jetson, half precision was the cheapest engine, not int eight, and on the arm it went nineteen for twenty.

18
00:01:36,758 --> 00:01:40,280
The audit's top picks did no better than random picks.

19
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:43,410
A new can worked forty-eight out of forty-eight.

20
00:01:43,410 --> 00:01:47,259
But with the start moved three centimeters, the arm failed.

21
00:01:47,259 --> 00:01:55,738
So we recorded fifty demonstrations at shifted positions, fine-tuned on Nebius, and tested it blinded, at a position it never saw.

22
00:01:55,738 --> 00:02:03,239
There, it went twelve for twelve, where the old policy went three for twelve, and ten for twelve on the other side.

23
00:02:03,239 --> 00:02:06,500
At the trained spot, more trials are still needed.

24
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,519
The A I layers get checked too.

25
00:02:09,519 --> 00:02:16,246
In its first setup, Cosmos Reason, on a Nebius A I Endpoint, caught one failure in twenty-one.

26
00:02:16,246 --> 00:02:24,549
We tested it like any other component, on held-out recordings, and a validated question caught twenty of twenty-one.

27
00:02:24,549 --> 00:02:32,350
Nemotron Super on Token Factory explains the review in words, while every verdict and number comes from code.

28
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:35,222
So QuantGate answers three questions.

29
00:02:35,222 --> 00:02:37,724
Which candidate do I validate first?

30
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:38,767
Half precision.

31
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:40,157
Which do I distrust?

32
00:02:40,157 --> 00:02:48,289
Broken calibrations, which a simple input check catches, and any engine whose files no longer match what was audited.

33
00:02:48,289 --> 00:02:50,027
Can I approve deployment?

34
00:02:50,027 --> 00:02:50,583
Not yet.

35
00:02:50,583 --> 00:02:54,475
The review lists exactly which trials are still missing.

36
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:55,975
Quant Gate.

37
00:02:55,975 --> 00:02:58,750
Earn the next test, not the approval.
